From b66a7e3071990a991d1d0a9682cc05f177f969f0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bare <git@barebrowser.org>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 03:43:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Let bookmarks be carried in and out as a file

Bookmarks were the one thing a person arriving from another browser could
not bring with them, and the one thing they could not take away again.
Every browser reads and writes the same file for this, so the work was
not to invent anything but to reach what the browser already has.

Both halves were already here and already compiled into this build. The
file is parsed by the same code the desktop importer uses, in a sandboxed
process because it came from somewhere else; it is written by the same
writer that produces the bookmarks.html Firefox, Chrome and Safari read.
Neither needed changing to work from a document the user picked: a content
URI is a FilePath on Android, and opening one goes through the system
picker's grant, so the browser sees the one document and nothing else. No
permission is asked for and none is needed.

What was missing was the step in between. The routine that puts parsed
bookmarks into the model lives in chrome/browser/importer, which asserts
it is not being built on Android, and it is unbuildable here only because
the rest of that class writes passwords and history and search engines.
The bookmark part needs a model and nothing else, so it moves to where the
bookmarks live and the desktop importer calls it there. It is the same
code, so it keeps the same behaviour on both.

That behaviour is what makes importing safe: it only ever adds. The file
is read and understood in full before anything is written, so a file that
turns out to be unreadable, or to be an ordinary web page, costs nothing.
Nothing existing is removed, emptied or rewritten, which is also why
importing the same file twice leaves two copies rather than none. The
same page can reasonably be filed in two places and there is no way to
tell an unwanted duplicate from a deliberate one, so this does not guess.
